Kotlin's `coroutineScope` is a clean, direct expression of the rule:

```kotlin
suspend fun fetchDashboard(): Dashboard = coroutineScope {
    val user = async { fetchUser() }
    val posts = async { fetchPosts() }
    val stats = async { fetchStats() }
    // coroutineScope will NOT return until all three complete
    Dashboard(user.await(), posts.await(), stats.await())
}
```

The three `async` calls all start concurrently — this is the same fan-out win `async-await-and-cooperative-concurrency` covered — but the containing `coroutineScope` block is the load-bearing part: it cannot return until `user`, `posts`, and `stats` have all completed, one way or another. There's no code path out of this function that leaves one of them still running unaccounted for; the scope itself won't allow it.
